  "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,4]$ and differentiable on $(1,4),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (1,4)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=1$ and $b=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{log}_{2}\u20614-2{log}_{2}\u20611}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-0}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{log}_{2}\u2061x)=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(c)=\\frac{2}{c\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{c\\operatorname{ln}\u20612} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}\u20612} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ c\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
